# Air Isolator — Hygienic Diaphragm Barrier for Sip-and-Puff Input

**Project:** SipNPuff (github.com/mkadie/SipNPuff)
**Status:** Design note / proposal → **manuscript-in-progress**
**Scope:** A cheap, user-replaceable membrane that isolates the user's breath (moisture, saliva, pathogens) from the pressure sensor and electronics, suitable for our device and reusable across other open-source assistive projects. Sip-and-puff is a long-established breath-pressure access method for users with severe mobility impairment [1, 2].

**Target venue:** **HardwareX** (Elsevier; peer-reviewed open-access, open-source hardware; ISSN 2468-0672) — see the *HardwareX submission map* at the end. Backup: *Journal of Open Hardware*. As a standalone open-hardware article, this is the archival home for the isolator contribution; the SipNPuff paper should **cite** it rather than re-publish it (one archival home per contribution).

### Abstract (draft — validation results pending bench tests)

Breath-actuated assistive devices (sip-and-puff switches) expose their pressure sensors to saliva, condensate, and pathogens, yet hygienic isolation is rarely addressed in do-it-yourself and low-cost assistive technology. We present an open-source, 3D-printable *air isolator*: a few-cent, latex-free, user-replaceable elastomer membrane (nitrile finger cot or non-latex dental dam) that fully separates the breath path from the pressure sensor, providing both sensor protection and between-user infection control. The design adapts the medical pressure-transducer diaphragm principle for fused-filament fabrication and common medical consumables, and includes a sealed glue-in "Unit" variant, a screw-together (cot-replaceable) Unit, and a ball-and-socket joystick variant. The central design constraint is membrane tension: a slack, rolling-diaphragm geometry is predicted to preserve the soft-sip/soft-puff sensitivity that a taut membrane degrades. We *[will]* quantify this slack-vs-taut-vs-direct tradeoff and thermal-drift behavior on the bench, and we report low-volume fabrication cost (~$2.10/unit). All design files, bill of materials, and a parametric cost model are released under an open license for reuse across assistive-technology projects.

> *Drafting note:* the italicized claims above become results only after the bench characterization (see submission map, Validation). HardwareX requires demonstrated operation and characterization — this is the gating work before submission.

