---

## Concept

A flexible membrane separates the "dirty" breath path from a sealed "clean" air pocket facing the pressure sensor. Sip/puff pressure deflects the membrane, which transmits the pressure into the clean pocket without any breath, condensate, or saliva touching the sensor. This is the same diaphragm-isolation principle used in medical pressure transducers to keep a sensor sterile across patients, so the approach is proven — the engineering is in getting the membrane compliance, sealing, and drift handling right.

The barrier serves two goals at once:

1. **Protect the electronics** — keep the MEMS pressure sensor dry and clean for the life of the device.
2. **Infection control** — provide a full physical barrier between the user and the reusable hardware, with a swappable consumable for hygiene between sessions or users. Disposable inline barriers are standard practice on breath-driven medical devices to prevent cross-infection and protect equipment [3, 4].

---

## Critical design rule: the membrane must be slack, not taut

A tensioned membrane has its own spring constant that adds directly to the pneumatic system. It resists deflection, kills sensitivity on **soft sips/puffs** (exactly the thresholds that are already hardest to detect reliably), and introduces hysteresis. Use a **loose / rolling-diaphragm geometry** so the membrane contributes near-zero stiffness over the working range and simply floats the pressure across. Slack beats taut every time.

---

## Architecture

Make the entire dirty path a single snap-in **consumable front module**:

```
[ mouthpiece ] -- [ tube ] -- [ chamber w/ membrane ] || [ clean pocket + sensor ]
\___________ replaceable consumable ___________/         \___ permanent, stays clean ___/
```

- **Puff** (positive pressure) pushes the membrane toward the clean side → compresses the trapped pocket → sensor reads positive.
- **Sip** (negative pressure) pulls the membrane away → trapped pocket expands → sensor reads negative.
- Both directions work with one membrane and one sealed pocket.

The sensor and PCB never see breath. Only the cheap front module is ever replaced.

---

## Membrane material selection

**Hard requirement: latex-free.** Latex allergy is a serious liability in an assistive-tech context where users have prolonged oral contact; an estimated 8–12% of regularly exposed health-care workers become sensitized, and natural-rubber-latex devices (including balloons and condoms) are well-documented allergens [5, 6, 7]. The condom option (raised earlier) is dropped — same physics is available from cleaner-to-source, lower-friction materials below.

**Watch out:** most "latex-free balloons" sold for parties are **Mylar / foil (PET film)** and are *not* elastic — they will not work as a pressure diaphragm. Genuinely elastic non-latex materials are **nitrile, polyisoprene, chloroprene, and polyurethane**.

### Recommended (in order of practicality)

1. **Nitrile finger cot — best "balloon" analog.**
   Pre-rolled, ~0.08 mm (≈3 mil) thin, latex-free and powder-free, and dirt cheap in bulk. The rolled open end is effectively a self-sealing balloon neck: roll it onto a printed cylindrical boss/barb and it seals mechanically with zero adhesive, while staying tool-free to swap. This is the closest thing to the "balloon" idea without latex and without the sourcing problems.

2. **Non-latex dental dam sheet (polyisoprene or nitrile).**
   Flat elastic sheet — ideal for a clamp-ring / O-ring mounting design rather than stretch-over-a-barb. Medical-grade, tear-resistant, available in thin/medium gauges (pick thinner for lower stiffness). Polyisoprene and nitrile versions both exist.

3. **Elastic non-latex balloon (only if you specifically want a balloon form).**
   Use **chloroprene** ("cloudbuster"-type) or **polyurethane** balloons, or Qualatex-style stretchy-plastic "Bubbles." Avoid anything foil/Mylar.

