## Mounting & sealing (3D-printed housing)

Printed plastic leaks through layer lines, so do not rely on the print alone for the seal. See **[`air-isolator-geometry.svg`](./air-isolator-geometry.svg)** for both mounting options in cross-section.

- **Stretch-over-a-barb (preferred for finger cot / balloon):** print a cylindrical boss; roll the membrane's rolled edge onto it. Self-sealing like a balloon on its own neck, and tool-free to replace. Add a shallow retention groove or bead so it can't creep off under repeated sip suction.
- **Clamp ring / O-ring (preferred for dental-dam sheet):** capture the flat sheet between a printed ring and the body with an O-ring in a groove. Gives a clean, leak-tight clamp around the full perimeter.
- Keep the membrane slack in either case — leave slack length so it can roll/float rather than stretch.

### Fallback: sealed glue-in "Unit"

If neither the stretch-over-boss nor the clamp/O-ring approach seals reliably or repeatably, don't fight it — the printed plastic is cheap enough that the whole front module can become a single sealed, replaceable **Unit**. Insert the finger cot (or sheet), then bond the housing halves airtight (glue / solvent weld / gasketed snap) so the membrane seal is captured at assembly and never has to be field-serviced. The user swaps the entire low-cost Unit rather than re-seating a membrane.

This trades a tool-free in-field membrane swap for guaranteed sealing and dead-simple assembly. Given the per-Unit material cost is on the order of a finger cot plus a few grams of filament, treating the module as a disposable/replicable Unit is a perfectly acceptable — and arguably more robust — production path. Keep the membrane slack inside the Unit regardless. Cross-section: **[`air-isolator-glued-unit.svg`](./air-isolator-glued-unit.svg)**.

### Joystick variant

The same sealed Unit can ride on a ball-and-socket pivot so the mouthpiece doubles as a joystick: tilt moves the cursor (2-axis) while sip/puff through the same mouthpiece provides clicks. Mouth-operated joysticks and magnetic position sensing are established in assistive control — e.g., the magnetic Tongue Drive System, which uses an intra-oral magnet as a joystick analog [10]. Tilt is read contactlessly by a magnet on the ball over a 3-axis Hall sensor (no wear, sealed, low power — fits the RP2350 / Seesaw I²C path) [11, 12]; the sip/puff air runs through a flexible tube routed off-axis through the ball to the pressure sensor, and a centering boot recenters the stick and seals the socket. The Unit push-fits onto the stem and into the air tube, so hygiene swaps never touch the mechanism. Cross-section: **[`air-isolator-joystick.svg`](./air-isolator-joystick.svg)**.

---

## Electrical / firmware notes

- **Thermal drift:** warm, humid breath heats the trapped pocket and shifts the baseline. Handle with firmware **auto-zeroing / baseline tracking** on the sip/puff signal.
- **Optional slow bleed:** a very slow bleed on the *clean* side through a 0.2 µm hydrophobic PTFE filter lets DC drift escape to ambient while the dynamic sip/puff signal still registers and the barrier stays clean. Often unnecessary if firmware baseline tracking is solid — evaluate empirically.
- **Sensor referencing:** decide gauge vs. differential. For the sealed-pocket design, a gauge or differential sensor referenced to ambient (with the reference/bleed port on the clean side only) is the natural choice. (Open question — see below.)

---

## Alternative considered: off-the-shelf hydrophobic vent

A **hydrophobic PTFE vent** (Gore-style) directly over the sensor port passes gas while blocking liquid and bacteria, so it reads *true* pressure with zero added mechanical compliance and no diaphragm-stiffness problem [8, 9]. Inline spirometry/viral filters are a similar certified option [3, 4].

**Tradeoff:** it's a higher-cost per-unit consumable and is a liquid/pathogen barrier rather than a full physical wall between users. The membrane approach wins for the cheap, fully-isolating, user-replaceable goal; the vent is the lower-effort path if "keep the sensor dry" matters more than full between-user isolation. Worth keeping as a fallback / variant.

---

## Why this fits open-source assistive hardware

The consumable is a few cents (a finger cot or a dental-dam square), medical-grade, latex-free, and swappable with no tools. Any downstream project can adopt the same boss/clamp interface and source the membrane from common medical suppliers. The reusable electronics stay clean indefinitely. Open-source, locally 3D-printed assistive devices are a well-established model [13, 14, 15]; a comparable open-source 3D-printed orthosis reported roughly 1.5 h assembly and ~$15 material cost [13], closely paralleling the per-Unit economics below.

---

## Cost (low-volume / 3D-printed)

Full line-item math, editable inputs, and scenario band live in **[`air-isolator-cost-model.csv`](./air-isolator-cost-model.csv)** — edit the INPUTS block there and recompute. Summary at mid-case assumptions:

- **≈ $2.10 per Unit**, **≈ $25 per plate of 12** (≈ $9.70 materials + ≈ $15.40 labor).
- **Labor is ~60% of cost; materials ~40%** (~$0.80/Unit materials). Bigger V0.1 parts (~26 g) raise filament cost versus the earlier estimate.
- Scenario band: **~$1.50 (low) – ~$2.10 (mid) – ~$3.00 (high)** per Unit. Optional machine amortization adds ~$0.20–0.25/Unit.
- Assumes $20/hr labor, batch ("plateful") assembly, ~12 Units per ~220×220 mm bed, ~4 h unattended print per plate, and a placeholder ~8 g part — **part mass and per-Unit assembly time are the two biggest uncertainties** until CAD and a real slicer estimate exist.

Practical takeaways: printing is unattended, so the only print-side labor is starting the job and harvesting the plate — which is exactly why batching by the plateful collapses per-Unit overhead. A printed assembly jig that holds the shell while seating the cot and driving the screws can pull the mid case toward ~$1.60. At ~$0.80/Unit material cost, the low plastic cost is what keeps the disposable-mouthpiece and replaceable-cot models viable.

> When the design firms up, update the CSV inputs (part mass from the slicer, measured assembly time) and these figures re-derive directly.

