## Mouthpiece hygiene strategy

The mouth-contacting surface is handled with a tiered strategy matched to the fabricator's capability, because this is open-source hardware built by makers of very different resources. The membrane and tubing remain the **primary** hygienic barrier, so the printed mouthpiece surface is a second-line, comfort-and-cleanliness concern — not the sole safety line.

Why tiered: FFF layer lines are porous and trap bacteria that ordinary washing (even a dishwasher) doesn't reliably remove, so a reused, untreated mouthpiece degrades hygienically over days-to-weeks. Coatings and biocompatible-SLA can fix the surface but depend on maker skill and quality control an open project can't enforce. So the baseline is a cheap, frequently-replaced disposable, with better finishes offered as optional tiers.

- **Baseline (all builds):** a small snap-on **disposable mouthpiece** in an easy, accessible plastic (PLA, or food-grade PETG/PCTG), washed between uses and replaced on schedule. Split the mouthpiece from the Unit/electronics so the disposable is a fraction of a gram — pennies and minutes to reprint.
- **Optional (capable hobbyist):** a reusable mouthpiece finished with brush/dip food-grade epoxy or a food-grade silicone cover/dip — documented, with a cure-verification step. Never mix epoxy and platinum silicone (amine cure inhibition). Hand-wash only.
- **Clinical / pro SKU:** a mouthpiece printed in **certified biocompatible SLA dental resin** with the validated wash + UV post-cure (biocompatibility is only valid if that process is followed).

**Replacement schedule (X), risk-tiered:**

- **Shared / clinical / multi-user:** single-patient use — never shared; replace per user or per session.
- **Personal, single user:** wash after each use; replace ~weekly (conservative) to biweekly.
- Rationale: colonization is time- and moisture-driven and invisible, so err short — the disposable costs pennies.

No printed or coated surface should be assumed dishwasher-safe (PLA/PETG/PCTG soften ~60–80 °C); hand-wash. This is design guidance, **not** a validated infection-control protocol — see disclaimer.

