## Open questions / next steps

- [ ] Pick membrane #1 form factor: finger-cot-on-boss vs. dental-dam-on-clamp. Prototype both.
- [ ] Define sensor referencing (gauge vs. differential) and whether the clean-side bleed is needed.
- [ ] Bench-test soft-sip / soft-puff threshold sensitivity with a slack membrane vs. direct (no isolator) to quantify any loss.
- [ ] Characterize thermal drift over a 60 s continuous-use window; tune auto-zero.
- [ ] Spec the consumable front-module geometry and a tool-free retention detail.
- [ ] Decide swap model: re-seatable membrane vs. sealed glue-in Unit. If field sealing is unreliable, default to the disposable Unit (insert cot → bond airtight) given low plastic cost.
- [ ] Confirm membrane material chemical/biocompatibility for oral-adjacent use (the membrane is the only mouth-facing surface).
- [ ] **Mouthpiece shell material & finish:** print the front shell in a transparent food-contact-grade copolyester (food-grade PCTG, e.g. Fiberlogy transparent, or a GN071-based PETG) with a stainless/hardened-steel nozzle; prefer transparent/natural colors (pigments/UV additives are usually untested for food contact). For true mouth-contact safety, seal with a brush/dip food-grade epoxy (e.g. ArtResin, MAX CLR) once fully cured, or fit a food-grade silicone cover — **hand-wash only, not dishwasher** (PLA/PETG/PCTG soften ~60–80 °C; coatings aren't heat-durable). Membrane + tubing remain the primary hygienic barrier, so the shell is not a food-critical surface.
- [ ] **Joystick — tube fatigue:** the flexible air tube flexes on every cursor move, all day. Spec a soft silicone tube with a generous service loop and large bend radius at the ball exit; make the tube part of the replaceable consumable path so a stiffened/cracked tube is a cheap swap, not a teardown.
- [ ] **Joystick — input cross-talk:** tilt and sip/puff are nominally independent, but a hard puff can nudge the stick and a hard tilt can perturb chamber pressure. Plan firmware decoupling (e.g., briefly freeze the cursor during a detected click; debounce pressure events against tilt velocity).

