## Path to FDA compliance / certification (what it would take)

This outlines what formal U.S. FDA compliance would involve, for anyone wanting to take the device beyond open-source/personal/research use into a regulated product. It is a **roadmap, not legal or regulatory advice** — consult a regulatory professional.

1. **Classification (driven by intended use).** FDA class depends on the stated intended use and indications. A powered AAC device like this most likely falls under **21 CFR 890.3710, "Powered communication system"** — a battery/AC-powered device for medical purposes used to transmit or receive information by persons unable to use normal communication methods due to physical impairment. That category is **Class II (special controls)** but is **exempt from 510(k) premarket notification**, subject to the limitations in 21 CFR 890.9. Confirm the actual classification/product code for your specific intended use in FDA's product classification database — changing the claimed purpose can change the class, and a sub-component (e.g., the sip/puff switch) may classify separately.
2. **Premarket submission (if not exempt).** A non-exempt Class I/II device needs a **510(k)** (substantial equivalence to a legally-marketed predicate); a novel low-to-moderate-risk device with no predicate uses a **De Novo** request; high-risk Class III needs a **PMA**. For the likely 890.3710 route, no 510(k) is required — but "exempt" is **not** "unregulated."
3. **General controls (apply even when 510(k)-exempt).** FDA **establishment registration** and annual **device listing** (with the annual establishment fee); the **Quality System Regulation** (21 CFR 820, transitioning to the ISO 13485-harmonized QMSR, effective 2026); **labeling** (21 CFR 801) and **UDI**; and **Medical Device Reporting** of adverse events — plus the exemption limitations of 890.9 (you may not exceed the intended use or materially change safety/effectiveness).
4. **Biocompatibility (the mouthpiece).** Patient-contacting materials require a biological evaluation per **ISO 10993-1** (cytotoxicity, sensitization, irritation for mucosal/skin contact). This is exactly where the mouthpiece material and finish matter: biocompatibility of a printed part depends on the material **and** its validated post-process, not just the resin — see the mouthpiece hygiene tiers.
5. **Supporting evidence.** Design controls / design history file, risk management (**ISO 14971**), and, for powered medical devices, electrical safety and EMC (**IEC 60601** family) plus any performance validation.
6. **Cost/effort reality.** Even the exempt path carries registration fees and quality-system overhead; a full 510(k) path adds FDA user fees (reduced for small businesses), testing, and typically regulatory consulting — realistically tens of thousands of dollars and many months. This is a core reason the open-source route deliberately makes **no** compliance claim.
7. **Other markets.** FDA is U.S.-only. The EU (**CE marking under the MDR**), Health Canada, and Thailand's FDA (relevant to the Chiang Mai collaboration) each have separate frameworks. An academic or clinical partner's institution can often provide the quality/ethics umbrella that makes formal evaluation feasible.

**Bottom line:** for open-source/personal/research use the device operates **outside** any certified-product claim (see disclaimer). Formal FDA compliance is achievable — the likely AAC classification is even 510(k)-exempt — but still requires registration, a quality system, labeling, biocompatibility evidence for mouth-contact parts, and jurisdiction-specific work, best undertaken with a regulatory professional and an institutional partner.

